\title{Recruitment in FINN: the options and how to choose them}
\description{
Recruitment in FINN is set in several places: the regeneration process
function and its arguments in \code{\link[=createProcess]{createProcess()}} (or
\code{\link[=createHybrid]{createHybrid()}}), two arguments of \code{\link[=finn]{finn()}}, and the likelihood
and recruitment mode of \code{\link[=fit]{fit()}}, \code{\link[=predict.finn_class]{predict.finn_class()}} and
\code{\link[=simulateForest]{simulateForest()}}. This page says what each option does, how they act
together, and which combination to use for which purpose. The individual help
pages carry the full argument descriptions.

One simulated year of recruitment runs in three steps. The regeneration
process returns an EXPECTED recruitment per hectare for every site, patch and
species. This rate is turned into a whole number of recruits per patch, which
enter the stand as new cohorts at \code{finn(recruits_dbh = )}. During \code{\link[=fit]{fit()}}
the expected rate (not the drawn recruits), summed over an observation
interval, is compared with the observed \code{reg} by the regeneration likelihood.
}
\section{The recruitment process}{

The process is the \code{func} of the regeneration process passed to
\code{finn(regeneration_process = )}. All mechanistic variants share the same core:
a light response gated by the species parameter \code{parReg}, times the
environmental response \code{exp(env)}, plus the additive floor
\code{createProcess(reg_floor = )} of the regeneration process (default \code{1e-3}
stems per ha and year).
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=regeneration]{regeneration()}}: the core alone. The expected recruitment is not
bounded; it grows with the environmental response.
\item \code{\link[=regeneration_capped]{regeneration_capped()}}: the default since 0.2.0, used when
\code{regeneration_process} is \code{NULL}. The core is capped by a Beverton-Holt
term, \verb{K m / (K + m)}. \code{createProcess(cap = "all")} (default) gives one cap
\code{K} shared by all species, \code{cap = "species"} one per species. The cap carries
a prior, \verb{prior_lambda * (log K - log prior)^2}, with
\code{prior = "max_obs_recruits"} (the largest observed recruitment per ha and
year) and \code{prior_lambda = 0.1} by default. The cap bounds the MEAN
recruitment only. It does not bound the variance of the recruit draw, which
around a capped mean is still \code{K + K^2 / theta} (see the dispersion section
below).
\item \code{regeneration_saturation()}: the same function as \code{regeneration_capped()}
under its older name, kept as a deprecated alias for one release.
\item \code{\link[=regeneration_adult]{regeneration_adult()}}: the capped process with a seed source. The
recruitment of a species is multiplied by \code{f(A) = A / (A + A_half)}, where
\code{A} is the density of its own adults (stems above an adult threshold \code{x})
pooled over the patches of the site. A species without adults on a site
recruits only at \code{reg_floor}. \code{x} and \code{A_half} are declared through
\code{createProcess(custom_parameters = )}; see its help page.
}

A regeneration process built with \code{\link[=createHybrid]{createHybrid()}} replaces the whole
equation by a network. In 0.2.0 it has no Beverton-Holt cap: \code{createHybrid()}
accepts \code{cap}, \code{prior} and \code{prior_lambda}, but the network does not use them.
}

\section{From expected recruitment to recruits}{

The expected rate per ha times \code{patch_size} (in ha) is the expected number
of recruits per patch. \code{createProcess(sample_regeneration = )} decides how it
becomes a whole number:
\itemize{
\item \code{sample_regeneration = TRUE} (default): recruits are drawn from a negative
binomial with that mean and size \code{theta} (the dispersion, next section),
using \code{createProcess(regeneration_sampler = )}. \code{"refined"} (default since
0.2.0) draws the Gamma-mixed Poisson exactly where its rate is below 2 and
uses a Gaussian approximation above. \code{"gaussian"} reproduces results
published before 0.2.0; its guards act as a recruitment floor of 0.0011 per
cell and year (see \code{\link[=rnbinom_torch_refined]{rnbinom_torch_refined()}}).
\item \code{sample_regeneration = FALSE}: the expected number is used and rounded.
Wherever the expected number per patch is below 0.5 the rounding delivers
\strong{no recruit at all}. At small patches and low rates this is a silent zero
for every sparse species, however well its rate is calibrated.
}
}

\section{The recruit dispersion theta}{

\code{par_theta_recruits} is the negative-binomial size \code{theta}, one value per
species. It sets the variance of the recruit draw, \code{mu + mu^2 / theta}, and
the width of the negative-binomial regeneration likelihood. Three arguments of
\code{\link[=createProcess]{createProcess()}} (and \code{\link[=createHybrid]{createHybrid()}}) control it:
\itemize{
\item \code{dispersion_parameter}: the starting value, or the fixed value when
\code{theta} is not trained. Default \code{1}.
\item \code{optimizeTheta}: \code{TRUE} (default) trains \code{theta}. \code{FALSE} keeps it at
\code{dispersion_parameter} for the whole fit, in the draw and in the
likelihood alike.
\item \code{theta_floor}: a lower bound applied wherever \code{theta} is read, trained or
not. Default \code{0.5} since 0.2.0; \code{0} or \code{NULL} removes it. A fixed
\code{dispersion_parameter} below the floor is raised to the floor.
}

With the default \code{fit(rb_gradient = TRUE)}, \code{theta} is set only by the
regeneration likelihood. On sparse, zero-heavy recruitment data the
likelihood optimum of \code{theta} is very small: a small \code{theta} widens the
likelihood and so lowers the loss without improving the predicted mean, and
the same small \code{theta} makes the simulated draws bursty. A free \code{theta} can
therefore run away (FINNverse/FINN#63).

What the floor does: it keeps the fit stable, and in a fit run to
convergence it costs nothing. On the German national forest inventory, a full
model fitted for 2000 epochs with the floor predicted the recruitment level on
a spatially blocked holdout at 1.06 times the observed, a fixed \code{theta} of 1
(\code{optimizeTheta = FALSE}) at 1.04, and the two scored the same recruitment
likelihood and the same skill on every other response. The difference is in
how fast they get there: after 50 epochs a floored \code{theta} still put the level
about 12 times too high, a fixed \code{theta} 2 to 3 times, and a free \code{theta} had
run away by four orders of magnitude. So read the recruitment level only once
the parameters have settled (\verb{$param_history}), whichever setting you use; for
short fits, a fixed \code{theta} gets the level right sooner.
}

\section{The likelihood and the observation operator}{

\code{fit(loss_family = c(regeneration = ))} chooses the regeneration likelihood.
\code{"nbinom"} (default) uses \code{theta} as above. \code{"poisson"} has no dispersion
parameter and so cannot collapse; \code{theta} then only shapes the recruit draw.

\code{finn(recruit_obs_weight = w)} is an observation operator: it maps the
predicted and the observed recruitment per ha alike into the count space of
the sampling design before they are compared, so that the likelihood sees
the counts that were actually tallied. For an angle-count inventory
\code{w = g(recruits_dbh) / BAF}. For a fixed-radius small-tree plot that counts
the stems in a diameter band below the recruitment threshold,

\verb{w = plot area (ha) x residence time in the band (years) / observation period (years)},

where the residence time is the band width divided by the diameter growth of
the stems in it. One tallied stem then stands for \code{1 / w} recruits per ha over
the observation period. The residence time is an assumption and should be
estimated from the data where possible: \code{w} scales every absolute recruitment
number the fit returns, though not the ranking between model configurations.

In 0.2.0 \code{recruit_obs_weight} is applied \strong{only} under \code{"nbinom"}. With
\code{loss_family["regeneration"] = "poisson"} (or \code{"mse"}, \code{"gaussian"}) the term
scores the per-ha values as they are and \code{recruit_obs_weight} has no effect,
without a warning; the Poisson variance then is that of a per-ha value, not of
the tallied count (FINNverse/FINN#65). The Poisson term adds
\code{fit(trees_offset = )} (default \code{0.01}) to the prediction.
}

\section{Feeding observed recruitment}{

\code{fit(loss_recruit = "observed")} drives the simulated stand with the observed
recruitment instead of the regeneration process. The observed \code{reg} divided
by \code{period_length} is the expected recruitment per ha and year in every year
of that interval; times \code{patch_size} it is drawn per patch by the recruit
sampler, as above. Growth, mortality and competition run and are trained as
usual. The regeneration term is weighted 0 and every regeneration parameter
(species parameters, environment response, cap, \code{theta}, custom parameters)
is frozen for that call. \code{predict()} and \code{simulateForest()} take
\code{recruits = "observed"} and the table \code{obs_recruits} to simulate the same way. With
observed recruitment the model is not an autonomous simulator: every
simulated year must lie in an observed interval, and bare-ground, succession
or projection runs are refused.

The intended use is a staged calibration when the inventory samples small
trees too sparsely to calibrate recruitment jointly with the rest. First fit
the demography (growth, mortality, competition) with
\code{fit(loss_recruit = "observed")}; then fit the regeneration process on its own, with
the other processes frozen (\code{optimizeSpecies}/\code{optimizeEnv = FALSE}, or
through \code{loss_weights}).

One caveat: the fed per-site count is a noisy estimate, and it is re-sampled
as if it were the mean. The observation noise of a sparse small-tree count
therefore enters the simulated stand. On the German national forest
inventory one tallied small tree stands for about 1 600 stems per ha per
decade, and the fed counts carry a variance comparable to that of the whole
stem-count column, which costs accuracy in the simulated stem counts.
}

\section{Choosing}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Default settings} (\code{regeneration_capped}, trained \code{theta} with
\code{theta_floor = 0.5}, \code{"nbinom"}): the fit is stable, and once it has
converged the recruitment level is calibrated as well as with a fixed
\code{theta}. Do not read the level from a fit stopped early.
\item \strong{For short fits}, or when the recruitment level should not depend on how
long the fit runs: keep \code{theta} fixed (\code{createProcess(optimizeTheta = FALSE)}, with \code{dispersion_parameter} 1 or higher), or use
\code{fit(loss_family = c(..., regeneration = "poisson"))}. Mind that
\code{recruit_obs_weight} is ignored under \code{"poisson"} (above).
\item \strong{When small trees are sampled too sparsely}, and in particular when the
spatial pattern of recruitment is not predictable from the predictors (on
the German national forest inventory no configuration ranked sites better
than a Spearman correlation of about 0.08 out of sample): use the staged
route of the previous section.
\item \strong{Check before interpreting recruitment}: compare the predicted and the
observed total recruitment, and the regeneration loss in \code{m$history}
against its intercept-only baseline (with \code{weights = "auto"}, a value of 1
is no better than predicting the mean).
}
}

